## Model card (documented absence — no first-party artifact to score)

- **Name:** Grok 5 (a.k.a. `opencode/grok-5` — non-resolving placeholder id)
- **Short description:** xAI's **announced-in-principle but unreleased** next flagship. As of 2026-11-01 it exists only as **roadmap speculation** (Musk has cited ~6T parameters and shifting Q1-2026 → mid/late-2026 target dates that all slipped), not as a documented product. The curated meta states it accurately: "**xAI's next flagship is unreleased and unannounced: no model id, price, context, modalities or benchmarks exist on any xAI surface**," with the id note "**xAI has never published a 'Grok 5' as a model, id or preview. The current flagship is Grok 4.7.**"
- **Provider / access:** **None.** `docs.x.ai/developers/models` (fetched this pass, 2026-11-01) contains **no grok-5 row, no grok-5 model page.** No Artificial Analysis page, no OpenRouter listing, no HF repo exists for "Grok 5."
- **Release / knowledge:** **Unreleased.** xAI's most recent flagship is **Grok 4.7** (released 2026-09-21; 500K ctx; $2/$6 per 1M; May-2026 knowledge cutoff). Grok 5 has no release date, no knowledge cutoff.
- **IDs:** **None published.** `opencode/grok-5` is a dataset placeholder that does not resolve.
- **Context window:** **Not announced** (no vendor figure).
- **Modalities:** **Not announced.**
- **Pricing:** **Not announced** — no API, no hosted route. (Third-party "estimates" exist on SEO blogs; none is a vendor price.)
- **Architecture:** **Undisclosed.** Rumored ~6T-parameter / Colossus-2-trained flagship; no xAI tech report or model card to verify.

### Raw benchmarks found

> **None.** No vendor benchmark, no Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index / Speed / Cost measurement, and no neutral harness (Vals / Epoch / swebench.com / tbench.ai / LMArena) has any "Grok 5" result — because the model does not exist as a callable or documented artifact.

- **Primary evidence (official):** **xAI docs `developers/models`, fetched 2026-11-01** — full Text-API pricing table lists only grok-4.7 / 4.6 / 4.5 / 4.3 / 4.20-* / build-0.1; **no grok-5.** Selection guide: *"Code: Grok 4.7 · Chat: Grok 4.7"*; note: *"Grok 4.7 … is the most capable model we've built."* → the flagship slot Grok 5 would occupy is **still held by 4.7.**
- **Corroborating independent evidence:** **ZBuild status tracker** (2026-10-11 update): "As of October 11, 2026, Grok 5 is **not in SpaceXAI's API model list, pricing page or release notes**, and there is no announcement on x.ai. The current flagship is Grok 4.7." **presenc.ai lineage survey**: "The current Grok model is Grok 4.7, released on September 21, 2026." Multiple trackers (lumichats, geotoolbox, computeunion) uniformly report Grok 5 as **in-training / unreleased with speculative, non-official pricing.**
- **The lone contradicting page is rejected:** **allthings-elon.com** (2026-10-31, "xAI Opens Grok 5 to Developers") — a generic prose piece with **no model id, no numbers, no card link**, whose "API now live / debut" framing is **flatly contradicted by xAI's own live model list** (still 4.7, no grok-5) fetched the same day. Treated as an SEO/content-farm artifact, **not** a first-party reveal; **no specs can be extracted from it to score.**

> Provenance / conclusion: **a documented absence, not a phantom SKU and not a distinct artifact.** All prongs of the self-exclusion rule are met: no first-party model card, no id, no weights, no endpoint, and **zero independently verifiable benchmarks.** The curated meta's own tradeoff concurs verbatim: "*Nothing to evaluate — the folder's findings are a self-exclusion until xAI publishes.*" Scoring would require inventing numbers or importing **Grok 4.7's** specs (a reattribution of a *different, separately-queued* model — see the live grok-4.7 folder).
> Recommended curator resolution: **keep `grok-5` parked / unscored.** **Only when** xAI publishes a real `grok-5` model id / card on docs.x.ai (or a neutral harness evaluates a grok-5 endpoint with first-party numbers) should this `.md.excluded` be replaced by a scored `Qwen_3.8_Max.md` researched independently against that release — and even then it must **not** borrow Grok 4.7's benchmarks.

